4. Image version control strategy

This is asking:

How do we know exactly which version of the Golden AMI is running?

Imagine you have:

Golden Windows 2022

v1.0.0
v1.0.1
v1.0.2
v1.1.0
v2.0.0

You need to be able to answer:

"What changed between v1.0.1 and v1.0.2?"

Your versioning system should therefore capture:

Version
    ↓
Source AMI
    ↓
OS version
    ↓
Patch level
    ↓
Installed software
    ↓
Security baseline
    ↓
Build date
    ↓
Build ID
    ↓
Test results
    ↓
Approval
Example
Golden AMI
Windows Server 2022
Version: 1.2.3
Build Date: 2026-09-01
Patch Level: September 2026
Security Baseline: v3.2
Monitoring Agent: 8.x
EDR: Approved Version

Then your documentation should say:

Every Golden AMI must have a unique immutable version and corresponding build metadata.

Why immutability matters

You don't want this:

AMI v1
   ↓
Someone modifies it
   ↓
Still called v1

Instead:

AMI v1
   ↓
Change required
   ↓
Build v2

This gives you traceability and makes rollback possible.

AWS Image Builder recipes use semantic versions and each pipeline build creates a new image version. Image Builder lifecycle policies can then manage older versions.

5. Testing and QA requirements before release

This is one of the most important sections.

You need a release gate.

A Golden AMI shouldn't become "production approved" just because the Image Builder pipeline succeeded.

I'd structure your QA as:

                 AMI Build
                    │
                    ▼
              Build Successful?
                    │
             ┌──────┴──────┐
             │             │
            NO            YES
             │             │
           FAIL            ▼
                    OS Validation
                         │
                         ▼
                    Security Scan
                         │
                         ▼
                    Agent Testing
                         │
                         ▼
                    Functional Test
                         │
                         ▼
                    Compliance Test
                         │
                         ▼
                    QA Approval
                         │
                         ▼
                  Production Release
