6. Rollback procedures

This section answers:

What do we do if the new Golden AMI breaks something?

For example:

                  AMI v2
                    │
                    ▼
                 Release
                    │
                    ▼
             Production issue
                    │
                    ▼
             Rollback decision
                    │
                    ▼
               AMI v1

The most important concept is:

Never overwrite the previous known-good AMI.

Instead:

v1.0.0 = Known good
v1.0.1 = New release

If v1.0.1 fails:

Production
    ↓
Rollback
    ↓
v1.0.0
What should your rollback procedure cover?
1. Identify failed version
AMI: ami-xxxxxxxx
Version: 1.0.1
2. Stop new deployments

Prevent Terraform/CloudFormation/Launch Templates from using the bad version.

3. Restore previous AMI reference

For example:

Launch Template

AMI:
ami-OLD-KNOWN-GOOD
4. Redeploy

For Auto Scaling environments:

Launch Template
      ↓
Previous AMI
      ↓
New instances
5. Investigate

Determine:

What changed?
Why did testing miss it?
Is the AMI permanently rejected?
Does the next version need a fix?
Important point

Your rollback strategy should not simply be "restore the old AMI."

You also need to know where the AMI is referenced.

Potential references include:

Terraform
CloudFormation
Launch Templates
Auto Scaling Groups
EKS nodes
AWS Batch
Custom deployment scripts

So your KB should include:

Before retiring an AMI, verify that no active infrastructure references it.

7. Documentation standards

This is where everything comes together.

Every Golden AMI release should have a record.

I'd recommend maintaining an AMI Release Record.

Example:

Field	Example
AMI Name	golden-windows2022
Version	1.2.0
AMI ID	ami-xxxxxxxx
Source AMI	ami-yyyyyyyy
OS	Windows Server 2022
Patch Level	September 2026
Build Date	2026-09-01
Image Builder Pipeline	windows-golden-ami
Security Scan	PASS
QA	PASS
Approval	Approved
Regions	us-east-1, us-west-2
Status	Active
I would also add a Change Log

This is extremely useful.

Version	Date	Changes	Test Result	Status
1.0.0	Aug 2026	Initial baseline	PASS	Retired
1.0.1	Sep 2026	OS security patches	PASS	Active
1.0.2	Sep 2026	Agent update	FAIL	Rejected
1.0.3	Sep 2026	Agent update fixed	PASS	Active

This gives you a complete audit trail.

