One important distinction for your ticket

There are actually two different maintenance activities you should document:

A. Maintaining the Golden AMI
Golden AMI
    ↓
Patch
    ↓
Build NEW AMI
    ↓
Test
    ↓
Release
B. Maintaining already-running EC2 instances
Running EC2
    ↓
Patch Manager
    ↓
Patch
    ↓
Compliance check

Don't mix these together in the KB.

AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager can patch running managed nodes and report their compliance, while Image Builder is used to produce new image versions.

So your overall architecture could eventually be:

                 GOLDEN AMI PIPELINE
                        │
               EC2 Image Builder
                        │
             ┌──────────┴─────────┐
             │                    │
          Build                 Test
             │                    │
             └──────────┬─────────┘
                        │
                      AMI
                        │
                Distribution
                        │
             ┌──────────┼──────────┐
             ▼          ▼          ▼
           Dev         QA        Prod
             │          │          │
             └──────────┼──────────┘
                        │
                 Running EC2 Fleet
                        │
                        ▼
                SSM Patch Manager
                        │
                        ▼
                Patch Compliance

That separation will make your final KB much more professional.

And for the retirement side, remember: deprecation hides an AMI from normal selection but does not stop existing launch references or delete the AMI; actual cleanup requires deregistration, and Image Builder lifecycle policies can automate the lifecycle stages.

If you're building this KB step-by-step, I would tackle Section 2.3 "Recommended Patching Frequency" next, because that's where you need to make an actual recommendation rather than simply describe AWS functionality.
